<h2>Full dentures</h2>
<p>When all of the natural teeth in an arch are missing, a full denture replaces them. Modern materials and digital workflow let us build dentures that look natural — not the bulky, obvious-looking dentures of decades past.</p>

<h2>Partial dentures</h2>
<p>If you still have several healthy teeth, a partial denture fills in the gaps without removing or grinding down what you have. Partials use small clasps or precision attachments to stay in place.</p>

<h2>The fitting process</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Initial exam and treatment planning</li>
  <li>Precision impressions and bite registration</li>
  <li>Try-in visits to perfect shade and tooth position</li>
  <li>Final delivery and follow-up adjustments to fine-tune fit</li>
</ul>

<h2>Care for your dentures</h2>
<p>Clean your dentures daily, soak them overnight, and bring them in for an annual exam so we can check the fit and inspect your gums. A poorly-fitting denture causes sore spots and accelerates jawbone loss — both worth catching early.</p>

<p><strong>Want a more stable solution?</strong> Many denture wearers eventually choose to upgrade to <a href="/services/implant-supported-dentures/">implant-supported dentures</a> or <a href="/services/all-on-4/">All-on-4 / All-on-X</a>. We'll happily walk you through every option at your appointment.</p>
